---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SEO from '@/components/SEO.astro';
import PageHeader from '@/components/PageHeader.astro';
import Section from '@/components/Section.astro';
import Callout from '@/components/Callout.astro';
import RelatedPages from '@/components/RelatedPages.astro';
import { SITE } from '@/data/site';

const path = '/about/';
const updated = new Date('2026-09-14');

const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { label: 'About', href: path },
];
---

<BaseLayout>
  <SEO
    title="About HeatPumpAtlasUSA"
    description="Who runs this site, how we make money, and why we publish the cases where a heat pump is the wrong financial answer."
    path={path}
    type="AboutPage"
    breadcrumbs={crumbs}
    updated={updated}
    primaryKeyword="about heatpumpatlasusa"
  />

  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="About"
    title="A heat pump cost site that publishes the losing cases"
    standfirst="Most heat pump content in the US is written to produce a lead. That produces a predictable bias, and the bias is the reason homeowners distrust the category."
    breadcrumbs={crumbs}
    updated={updated}
  />

  <div class="container-atlas py-10 lg:py-14">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl space-y-12">
      <div class="prose-atlas">
        <h2>The problem we are trying to solve</h2>
        <p>
          Search for a heat pump cost in the US and you will land on one of three things: a national average range copied
          from another site, a manufacturer page written by a marketing department, or a lead marketplace that wants your
          phone number before it tells you anything.
        </p>
        <p>
          Almost none of them will tell you that in your state, with your current fuel, a heat pump may not save you
          money. That is the most important thing a homeowner in this category needs to know, and the market has a
          structural reason not to say it.
        </p>

        <h2>Who writes this</h2>
        <p>
          Pages are written by the HeatPumpAtlasUSA editorial team and reviewed against the same published sources the
          calculator uses: EIA prices, NOAA climate data, Census fuel mix, and the administrator page for every rebate we
          list. We are not an HVAC contractor and we do not sell equipment. The point of that distance is so the model
          can say when a heat pump loses, not only when it wins.
        </p>
        <p>
          Every article shows a written-by line, a last updated date, and a link to the{' '}
          <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a>. Rebate pages also carry a separate verification date. If a number
          changes, the date changes with it.
        </p>

        <h2>What we do differently</h2>
        <p>
          We built a cost model that prices heat per million BTU rather than quoting a national average. It uses state
          electricity and gas prices from the EIA, heating degree days from NOAA, and heating fuel distribution from the
          Census. Then it publishes the result, including the states where the incumbent system wins.
        </p>
        <p>
          We also treat rebate status as a first-class field rather than a footnote. Several of the largest US programs
          have exhausted their funding and paused applications, and a lot of content still advertises that money as
          available. We label it instead.
        </p>

        <h2>What we do not do</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>We do not publish a page for every US city. Our own research found the search demand is not there.</li>
          <li>We do not scrape a national incentive database and republish it. We verify a focused list instead.</li>
          <li>We do not quote the expired federal 25C tax credit as if it were current.</li>
          <li>We do not tell you a heat pump always saves money. Sometimes it does not.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>How this site makes money</h2>
        <p>
          Through installer referrals in a small number of markets where we can verify coverage. A homeowner who runs the
          calculator can choose to send their scenario to installers who work in their ZIP code, and those installers pay
          for the introduction.
        </p>
        <p>
          That model only works if the recommendations are honest. A site that talks homeowners into bad projects gets a
          few months of revenue and then nothing, because the installers stop buying and the homeowners stop coming back.
          Publishing the losing cases is not a moral position here, it is the commercially sensible one.
        </p>

        <h2>What we are not</h2>
        <p>
          We are not an installer, we do not sell equipment, and we do not do site visits. We are not a substitute for a
          load calculation by a competent contractor. The{' '}
          <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a> sets out exactly what our model knows and what it cannot know, and
          the second list is longer.
        </p>

        <h2>Editorial standards</h2>
        <p>
          Every page carries a last updated date. Rebate pages carry a separate verification date. Where we cite a
          source, we link it, and we mark primary government or program sources differently from secondary benchmarks.
          The same rules sit on the <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a> so you can check the model, not just
          the prose.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you find an error, particularly in a rebate amount or a rate, please tell us. We publish corrections rather
          than quietly editing numbers.
        </p>
      </div>

      <Callout tone="note" title="Get in touch">
        <p id="contact">
          Corrections, data questions, and installer partnership enquiries go to the editorial desk. Use the button
          below. If you are a contractor and want to be considered for a market, tell us which ZIP codes you cover and
          how you handle heat pump sizing.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          <a
            href="/about/#contact"
            data-contact
            data-user="editor"
            data-domain={SITE.domain}
            class="text-brand-700 underline decoration-brand-200 underline-offset-2"
          >
            Email editorial
          </a>
        </p>
        <script>
          const link = document.querySelector<HTMLAnchorElement>('a[data-contact]');
          if (link?.dataset.user && link.dataset.domain) {
            link.href = `mailto:${link.dataset.user}@${link.dataset.domain}`;
          }
        </script>
      </Callout>

      <RelatedPages
        siblings={[
          { label: 'Methodology', href: '/methodology/', description: 'How every number is produced' },
          { label: 'Calculator', href: '/calculator/', description: 'The model, running' },
          { label: 'Markets', href: '/markets/', description: 'Where switching pays off' },
          { label: 'Rebates', href: '/rebates/', description: 'Program status' },
        ]}
      />
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
